[38;5;231m⏺[39m Happy to make the call. Here's the reasoning, because it changes what "notifications" even means.

  With a [1msingle user on one device, nobody else changes your tasks[0m — you do. So the literal brief ("notified when tasks
  they care about change") is close to vacuous there: you'd be notifying someone about their own keystrokes. What's
  genuinely useful in a single-user app is [3mtime- and rule-triggered[0m notification — this is due in an hour, this went
  overdue, this has sat untouched for two weeks. That's a real subsystem with real design questions (scheduling, dedupe,
  snooze, what happens while the tab is closed), and it's buildable.

  [1mMulti-user[0m is the only version where "someone changed a task you care about" is literally true, and it drags in
  accounts, authz, a server, and a sync transport before the first notification fires. That's a months-shaped project,
  not a feature.

  [1mMy suggestion: single-user, one device, notifications driven by time and rules.[0m Reasons: nothing in this repo suggests
  a team is waiting on this; it's the only option that produces something working soon; and the interesting design work
  (trigger rules, delivery, dedupe, the bell/inbox UI) is the same work you'd do in the multi-user version. I'd have
  the design write task mutations through a small append-only change log — that's the one seam that makes a later
  multi-user version an extension rather than a rewrite, and it costs almost nothing now.

  What I'd explicitly [3mnot[0m do: build accounts "just in case."
